{
  "rules": [
    "Strong negative bias across the full 30‑day horizon: model levels are consistently ~6–10 EUR/t below realized prices in all examples; apply an upward level shift (larger in the first ~20 days).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the recent history is rising, the model projects flattening/mean reversion, but the market continues trending up; increase sensitivity to recent momentum (especially days 1–15).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: errors are largest mid-horizon (roughly days 5–25) where the model stays near ~72–76 while outcomes run ~78–84; use a horizon ramp correction (bigger uplift mid-horizon, smaller at the very end).",
    "Late-horizon false downturn: model often predicts a sharp drop in the last week (e.g., down to high-60s/low-70s) that does not materialize; dampen late-horizon mean-reversion and cap downward slopes unless recent data already shows a selloff.",
    "Post-spike behavior: after a price spike in history, the model over-corrects downward (overshoots mean reversion); replace with slower decay—partial reversion only (e.g., revert 30–50% of the spike over 1–2 weeks, not fully)."
  ]
}
